feat(deploy): move the existing CA key into the store, keeping the same root (#238)

Rung 3 first minted a new root, which made adopting custody a MIGRATION:
every certificate already issued chains to the root the deployment already has,
and a new one would leave those chaining to something nobody serves. #238's
"same chain, same checks" is what forbids that, so the existing key moves
instead.

MEASURED END TO END against this deployment's real CA, not a fixture:

  the key imported into the store, wrapped, reported imported_key=true
  a transit signature VERIFIED under the existing certificate's public key,
    which is what proves the import preserved the key rather than any claim
    the store makes about itself
  Fulcio started on --ca=kmsca with no FATAL and served a root whose SHA-256
    fingerprint is byte-identical to the file CA's
  the CA's directory held a certificate and an issuer config and nothing else

Two one-shots, because the store's image has no openssl and the openssl image
has no store client. The plaintext key passes between them on a TMPFS volume
and never reaches a disk; both exit, and the volume goes with the project. It is
plaintext for the length of one import and in memory only — the same trust
boundary the encrypted key already had, since its password is in this
repository and has never been a secret.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO is undo prior exposure. The key's protection has been a
mount table and a published password; anything that already had a copy still
has one. Custody stops future reads. Rotation is what answers past ones, and
rung 3 is what makes rotation cheap: a store-generated key never has a password
to publish.

Two corrections the live run forced, both recorded where they bite. The pinned
store has DROPPED mlock and refuses to boot if its config mentions it, so the
disable_mlock line and the IPC_LOCK capability are gone rather than tuned — a
capability granted for a feature that no longer exists is the worst of both.
And `bao transit import` reads base64 DER, refusing PEM with "illegal base64
data at input byte 0".

Driven by OPS-050, whose adoption half is now asserted at unit level as well:
an existing root is adopted, an absent one mints, and an EMPTY one is refused
rather than minted over — a zero-byte ca.crt is a bootstrap in progress.

